														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1506471">
									

<p>It's not just pleasing people in chat. It's pleasing people on the SITE. Certain people seem to get a lot of positive votes because, typically, their writing seems to fall in line with what the majority of this site seems to like. Sometimes that happens instantly, sometimes it takes a few days. Certain people get a lot of downvotes because they write very, very well, and could make countless tales, but their writing is so prosaic and verbose it completely destroys any clinical tone which is what we strive for with the main SCP listings. And of course, some get downvoted into oblivion because they're horrible.</p>
<p>That said, a lot of it does come down to personal choice. Take my own products: I personally think that <a href="/scp-1320">SCP-1320</a> is better than <a href="/scp-767">SCP-767</a>, but 767 has a higher rating. Why? It clicks more with more people, that's all. That's how it is with every entry on this wiki.</p>
<p>I can't even say how many things I've seen on this site where half the discussion is posts saying how glorious it is, and the other half is saying how the universe is forever tainted by it having existed. But I CAN say that the community on this site is a passionate one, and a lot of folks tend to either hang out in IRC, or watch the &quot;recently posted&quot; page and Announcement threads like a hawk. That means a lot of new things get pounced on very quickly, and when the majority of people on the site think along the same lines&#8230;</p>

